" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606169} {:answer/id #uuid "1736fe7f-2a45-50c0-bbe3-6a0edd1035c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606182} {:answer/id #uuid "40300766-dfd8-52b5-ac01-c88c987d1a6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606278} {:answer/id #uuid "5695835b-9774-5044-bd06-861b5898e206" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606324} {:answer/id #uuid "bf62737b-bd83-5309-8998-d2cc88dcbf1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606534}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40300766-dfd8-52b5-ac01-c88c987d1a6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606278} :answer-field/id #uuid "a95d5df8-a929-54cb-9f32-3db118b598e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0ad01175-c452-5497-b4af-13f905c85f71" :question/math-academy-id "q-107552" :question/problem "Express $z=\\sqrt{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right))$ in the form $z=x+\\text{i}y.$" :question/worked-solution "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nFirst, we find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right)$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the values and expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\sqrt{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186606836 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28bc3958-c470-5c03-8eaf-90fb0a6b0d34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "29.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606229} {:answer/id #uuid "3bef2583-418a-513a-a849-37ecb870209e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606267} {:answer/id #uuid "4c2741a8-e634-5218-b11a-3ed147f17e09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606308} {:answer/id #uuid "53ad7cd8-51fb-5b2d-94b7-ea35a83ea9cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606319} {:answer/id #uuid "8bf1dfa6-c9cf-50ea-a343-44e64b6f202b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606437}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28bc3958-c470-5c03-8eaf-90fb0a6b0d34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "29.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606229} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd0d94eb-9966-5d50-8427-f9d9679debe1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4cf7e42e-ccd5-5593-8477-394afefbb0a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-95145" :question/problem "The average waiting time in a bank is $16\\,\\text{min}.$ Additionally, $25%$ of the customers are assisted in less than $11$ minutes. Assuming that the customer waiting times are normally distributed, find the percentage of customers that wait $20$ minutes or more, rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6664\\,$ | $\\,0.6700\\,$ | $\\,0.6736\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.7019\\,$ | $\\,0.7054\\,$ | $\\,0.7088\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7357\\,$ | $\\,0.7389\\,$ | $\\,0.7422\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent a customer's waiting time (in minutes). Then we have $X∼N(16,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X\\ge 20).$ But first, we need to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X<11)=0.25.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<11) & =0.25 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{11-16}{\\sigma }) & =0.25 \\\\ Φ\\left(-\\frac{5}{\\sigma }\\right) & =0.25 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.25)≈-0.6745.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{\\sigma } & =-0.6745 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =0.1349 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{0.1349} \\\\  & ≈7.4129.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X\\ge 20)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 20) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{20-16}{7.4129}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\ge 0.54) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<0.54) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.54) \\\\  & =1-0.7054 \\\\  & ≈29.5%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $29.5%$ of the customers wait $20$ minutes or more."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186606839 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14ac128f-6f15-5c3e-bd21-41e4209fad72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606174} {:answer/id #uuid "228c2182-00c9-50a4-9352-bdfc6ec33cf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606210} {:answer/id #uuid "34e3cd10-8ebb-59b7-8dd8-c63f449c7600" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4(\\sqrt{2}-1)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606253} {:answer/id #uuid "35833f57-6087-5dee-9639-866293481c9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16(2\\sqrt{2}-1)}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606254} {:answer/id #uuid "a5b11fe3-aadb-574a-9046-55ed056d13c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8(2\\sqrt{2}-3)}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606492}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35833f57-6087-5dee-9639-866293481c9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16(2\\sqrt{2}-1)}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606254} :answer-field/id #uuid "f86b7ea8-cf28-5f3d-be13-e58058b16444" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606790}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "605d11bf-b069-5d1f-86a2-ea2e3aec89d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-39160" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt[3]{y}}^{1}4\\sqrt{{z}^{4}+\\frac{x}{2}}\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx=$ \n\n*Hint: Change the order of integration to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.$*" :question/worked-solution "We cannot integrate the given integral because we cannot find a simple antiderivative of the integrand. So, we change the order of integration.\n\nWe wish to find the limits of integration ${u}_{1}(x,z),$ ${u}_{2}(x,z),$ ${v}_{1}(x),$ ${v}_{2}(x),$ $a,$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt[3]{y}}^{1}4\\sqrt{{z}^{4}+\\frac{x}{2}}\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}4\\sqrt{{z}^{4}+\\frac{x}{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,\\sqrt[3]{y}\\le z\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region. Since it is a type I region, we first sketch the projection onto the $xy$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nE=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/bf/bf06c344661079ef45437a6afd98be4e485ad300ba62eae91299a3773ca20cdb.png)\n\nThen, the region $R$ is shown in the diagram below, where $\\sqrt[3]{y}\\le z\\le 1.$\n\n![](images/9e/9ec0cfb27cf6b7ea47ccb010f2fd2238bbd0426b9dbd0035c8b78422ac32e4a4.png)\n\n \n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x,$ we write $R$ as a type III region and its projection $D$ onto the $xz$-plane as a type I *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $y$-axis and in the direction of increasing $y,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/fc/fcf5ded147f2937165c2f7a1a7d1f030103d64f6cb277fb09fb9049a21cf4b8c.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the left boundary and leaves through the right boundary:\n\n- The left boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\n- The right boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x,z),$ is\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt[3]{y}\\,⟹\\,y={z}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type III representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le {z}^{3}\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/2d/2dfeb845363b5721b936af1aadbffb47e55902fbe4db2c99894632e85405c0cf.png)\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type I plane region in the $xz$-plane with the bottom curve given by $z=\\sqrt[3]{0}=0.$\n\n![](images/90/90c6f2912371b5a2951a19bc4ae1fba827b98fdff6bbf73c18c2f8c139f53950.png)\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt[3]{y}}^{1}4\\sqrt{{z}^{4}+\\frac{x}{2}}\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{z}^{3}}4\\sqrt{{z}^{4}+\\frac{x}{2}}\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4\\sqrt{{z}^{4}+\\frac{x}{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{{z}^{3}}\\text{d}y]\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4\\sqrt{{z}^{4}+\\frac{x}{2}}[y]_{0}^{{z}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4{z}^{3}\\sqrt{{z}^{4}+\\frac{x}{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}4{z}^{3}\\sqrt{{z}^{4}+\\frac{x}{2}}\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{2}{3}{({z}^{4}+\\frac{x}{2})}^{3/2}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{(1+\\frac{x}{2})}^{3/2}-{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{3/2}]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{[\\frac{4}{5}{(1+\\frac{x}{2})}^{5/2}-\\frac{4}{5}{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{5/2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{15}{[{(1+\\frac{x}{2})}^{5/2}-{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{5/2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{15}[({2}^{5/2}-1)-(1-0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{15}[4\\sqrt{2}-2] \\\\  & =\\frac{16(2\\sqrt{2}-1)}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186606860 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "240bdc45-1153-59ee-be05-4f760b844003" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "63.6%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606213} {:answer/id #uuid "a45a0aec-59ef-5ac0-93b6-071570d52fad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72.5%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606489} {:answer/id #uuid "a8d529f8-50ef-5c86-bc3a-76d64000bf3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "67.4%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606498} {:answer/id #uuid "d4c48ffe-781d-533a-b574-c418a3db114e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "69.3%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606585} {:answer/id #uuid "e10966af-746f-55fa-adcc-1af0b5d3599f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "61.8%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606611}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8d529f8-50ef-5c86-bc3a-76d64000bf3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "67.4%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606498} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d512896-d27c-5600-8c7b-e855ff37a8cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606697}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8d67cbfd-0220-5994-aff5-cb754fd34cdc" :question/math-academy-id "q-95152" :question/problem "The duration of canine pregnancies is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu$ days and a standard deviation of $12$ days. Additionally, $10%$ of the pregnancies last less than $250$ days. Find the percentage of pregnancies with a duration greater than $260$ days.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.2946\\,$ | $\\,0.2912\\,$ | $\\,0.2877\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.3300\\,$ | $\\,0.3264\\,$ | $\\,0.3228\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.3669\\,$ | $\\,0.3632\\,$ | $\\,0.3594\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the length of a randomly selected pregnancy. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{12}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X>260).$ But first, we need to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X<250)=0.10.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<250) & =0.10 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{250-\\mu }{12}) & =0.10 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{250-\\mu }{12}\\right) & =0.10 \\\\ \\frac{250-\\mu }{12} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.10)≈-1.2816.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{250-\\mu }{12} & =-1.2816 \\\\ 250-\\mu  & =-15.3792 \\\\ \\mu  & =265.3792.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(260<X)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>260) & =P(Z>\\frac{260-265.3792}{12}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>-0.45) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-0.45) \\\\  & =1-0.3264 \\\\  & ≈67.4%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $67.4%$ of the pregnancies have a duration greater than $260$ days."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186606864 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ea30304-1835-56cb-a78b-faacbebbe838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606200} {:answer/id #uuid "2dd0477b-4437-5056-b28a-f815bbadcec5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606241} {:answer/id #uuid "696f8d92-2f2c-595b-a054-492416fe1aa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606362} {:answer/id #uuid "7c01b70f-4122-579e-bd63-e8f74a5a4809" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606402} {:answer/id #uuid "e73baa07-1da2-59a7-96d2-aa4655e0163b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606622}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "696f8d92-2f2c-595b-a054-492416fe1aa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606362} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea1ff431-b12f-5de7-a277-65e2f494aceb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "90ff7f30-27c5-5345-8dd7-ef5f83d36d8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-13839" :question/problem "What is the value of ${\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)}\\,\\text{d}x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4x}\\cdot 4=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡4$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡8$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|){|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡8)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{3})}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{2})}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186606871 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17942621-0c60-52af-9f73-dd155744244c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606183} {:answer/id #uuid "5d0f2f49-8b68-5d37-b745-74a5297c97b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606335} {:answer/id #uuid "869a6ff9-a74f-5f50-8757-26d3715e94b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606426} {:answer/id #uuid "9579906f-6548-5de1-a4d8-274a261efe99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606459} {:answer/id #uuid "e29a3fd2-e4b5-5002-b294-bbac8cb2be97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d0f2f49-8b68-5d37-b745-74a5297c97b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606335} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4866006-07b9-5003-8ad8-feed71447bb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606756}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a23c5957-18d7-5735-ae96-91f32a3b03e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-162533" :question/problem "Find the signed curvature of the curve $y=2\\sqrt{x}$ at $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The signed curvature $k(x)$ of a plane curve $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(x)=\\frac{{y}^{″}(x)}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}},\\,{y}^{″}(x)=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(x) & =\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}\\right)}{{[1+{\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\right)}^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}{(1+\\frac{1}{x})}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(1) & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{1}^{3}}{(1+\\frac{1}{1})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\cdot {2}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186606877 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "093180f8-3c11-5f7d-87bb-8bbc9f64ed35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606153} {:answer/id #uuid "4a0d439f-d4d3-5b2b-bb0f-7f853712eb09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606302} {:answer/id #uuid "d582cc7d-f1b3-5013-90af-eb883b31e154" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606586} {:answer/id #uuid "e20e29fa-124d-5061-bc76-4ca2e0893480" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606613} {:answer/id #uuid "fba2d1a0-5798-5545-825d-6851397fb7a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fba2d1a0-5798-5545-825d-6851397fb7a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606657} :answer-field/id #uuid "43838a59-7673-5419-8f48-08f821958cbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a6a85833-986f-5e25-859d-81b0f7195103" :question/math-academy-id "q-162531" :question/problem "Find the signed curvature of the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)$ at $x=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The signed curvature $k(x)$ of a plane curve $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(x)=\\frac{{y}^{″}(x)}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1},\\,{y}^{″}(x)=\\frac{2(1-{x}^{2})}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(x) & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{2(1-{x}^{2})}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\right)}{{[1+{\\left(\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\right)}^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-{x}^{2})}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}{[1+{\\left(\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\right)}^{2}]}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(0) & =\\frac{2(1-{0}^{2})}{({0}^{2}+1)^{2}{[1+{0}^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186606887 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04c5677d-95d5-50e5-a5bd-ad0e6f8f08e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=13(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.395)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.395))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606149} {:answer/id #uuid "20670ac2-d768-5e7a-bac8-ecf8a8103b1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.791)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.791)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606205} {:answer/id #uuid "2db8678a-939b-5716-8a2c-f62aee8baed7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=7(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.395)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.395))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606239} {:answer/id #uuid "7bab7816-1720-535e-a4ad-5188a6ddf2a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.395)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.395)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606401} {:answer/id #uuid "e52cccd9-598b-5955-80c0-dbe5cdef3f85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=13(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.791)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.791))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bab7816-1720-535e-a4ad-5188a6ddf2a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.395)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.395)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606401} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb4031cc-0da8-5afe-b73c-ec3875f34b89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bbc3926a-d6dc-51b2-8fa3-45bffbd0fbca" :question/math-academy-id "q-86607" :question/problem "Express $z=\\frac{13}{12-5\\text{i}}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$ Give $\\theta$ in radians to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\text{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $12+5\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{13}{12-5\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{13(12+5\\text{i})}{(12-5\\text{i})(12+5\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{13(12+5\\text{i})}{{12}^{2}-25{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{13(12+5\\text{i})}{144+25} \\\\  & =\\frac{13(12+5\\text{i})}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{13}+\\frac{5}{13}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=\\frac{12}{13}+\\frac{5}{13}\\text{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{12}{13}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{5}{13}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{144}{169}+\\frac{25}{169}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{144+25}{169}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{169}{169}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nIn polar form, $\\theta$  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=\\frac{12}{13}$ and $y=\\frac{5}{13}$ are both positive, $z$ lies in the first quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\left(\\frac{5}{13}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{12}{13}\\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{12}\\right) \\\\  & ≈0.395,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places. \n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.395)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.395)\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186606896 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4477310a-f45c-5c0d-8859-078598309ced" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(-20,60)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606292} {:answer/id #uuid "64021ae8-fe77-52b9-bb25-02a536cd98be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(-20,30)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606349} {:answer/id #uuid "c2524edd-878b-5684-a672-183bc974e860" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(-12,60)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606540} {:answer/id #uuid "d358f268-8533-5a3b-9c9a-15b32f08384f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(20,30)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606578} {:answer/id #uuid "e99995ba-e806-5be9-ae1d-6ac295e893fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(12,15)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606626}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4477310a-f45c-5c0d-8859-078598309ced" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(-20,60)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606292} :answer-field/id #uuid "25d6879c-068f-5bbc-8557-f5792168661f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d6c8e04c-f435-5624-a6dd-4b93b30508c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-143290" :question/problem "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15}$ are I.I.D random variables, where\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(-\\frac{4}{3},{2}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 15,\n$$\n\nwhat is the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{15}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed (I.I.D) normal random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}∼N(n\\mu ,\\,n{\\sigma }^{2}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15}$ are mutually independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{15} & ∼N(15\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{4}{3}\\right),\\,15\\cdot {2}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(15\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{4}{3}\\right),\\,15\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =N(-20,60).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186606999 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10ab2a05-15fd-5c6b-99e9-7ad72fdc04a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{4}(485)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606996} {:answer/id #uuid "290f96a7-7e16-597d-ab02-aff99eef0ad3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\log_{4}(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607000} {:answer/id #uuid "770cb98e-2052-576d-8f50-dea0c4df4726" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\log_{4}(15)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607008} {:answer/id #uuid "7ca1bdf1-00f7-56c8-aa2a-ea18d99635d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{4}(33)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607009} {:answer/id #uuid "d72e51aa-7ac1-5cad-a428-9fc998568c92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\log_{4}(12)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d72e51aa-7ac1-5cad-a428-9fc998568c92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\log_{4}(12)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607021} :answer-field/id #uuid "08dec1ad-b5bc-5826-b0fc-92869ff4b714" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606995}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "27098b03-0523-569d-af6b-5e8f6ddcb3ee" :question/problem "Express $4\\log_{4}(144) - 4\\log_{4}(12)$ as a single logarithm." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the quotient rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\log_n(a)-\\log_n(b)=\\log_n\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)\n$$\n\nIf we factor the given expression and use the quotient rule for logarithms, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\log_{4}(144)-4\\log_{4}(12)&=4\\bigl(\\log_{4}(144)-\\log_{4}(12)\\bigr) \\\\ &=4\\log_{4}\\left(\\frac{144}{12}\\right) \\\\ &=4\\log_{4}(12)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n4\\log_{4}(144)-4\\log_{4}(12)=4\\log_{4}(12)\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186607003 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44cbec5c-3129-51f7-82a6-e1bc1b26e0d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\log_{7}(8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607002} {:answer/id #uuid "ab976b3d-873c-5c73-b928-c17e1ca24287" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :d