-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06914b9e-76eb-5d5a-b29b-a128bd7b9fb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(158)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606994} {:answer/id #uuid "503c5b8a-eeb3-5700-836b-b902df96258f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\ln(9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607004} {:answer/id #uuid "670aea80-70fc-51ac-bc8e-0c9025db9b85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\ln(15)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607006} {:answer/id #uuid "6ade2824-d621-5c4d-966a-2f257c2daa06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(418)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607007} {:answer/id #uuid "b7a2439d-0a07-56bf-83cf-1ca68244fc12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\ln(14)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607017}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7a2439d-0a07-56bf-83cf-1ca68244fc12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\ln(14)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607017} :answer-field/id #uuid "013df6bd-5c50-5078-8a97-b0a2c7cb2fc3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bf204b7b-2775-507e-864a-a0e02bcef3a6" :question/problem "Express $3\\ln(98) - 3\\ln(7)$ as a single logarithm." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the quotient rule for natural logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\ln(a)-\\ln(b)=\\ln\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)\n$$\n\nIf we factor the given expression and use the quotient rule for logarithms, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\ln(98)-3\\ln(7)&=3\\bigl(\\ln(98)-\\ln(7)\\bigr) \\\\ &=3\\ln\\left(\\frac{98}{7}\\right) \\\\ &=3\\ln(14)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n3\\ln(98)-3\\ln(7)=3\\ln(14)\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186607026 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03585178-7dda-5452-8c44-e4dfd217949e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\log_{5}(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606993} {:answer/id #uuid "23f275a5-b47e-500e-a3d7-4d878974cfb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\log_{5}(15)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186606997} {:answer/id #uuid "7fae4088-c6b2-5b7e-b10d-212d685437f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\log_{5}(11)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607010} {:answer/id #uuid "a7dcb81b-1fd2-54fd-ae99-0330b526a2cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{5}(38)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607012} {:answer/id #uuid "b1844948-8d28-5316-b062-d08aacd07b1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{5}(12)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607015}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7fae4088-c6b2-5b7e-b10d-212d685437f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\log_{5}(11)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607010} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae9ae861-cc39-5082-b356-62f229f60d55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607014}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f850d55b-eade-51f5-ad1a-dc6accddd4b2" :question/problem "Express $5\\log_{5}(88) - 5\\log_{5}(8)$ as a single logarithm." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the quotient rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\log_n(a)-\\log_n(b)=\\log_n\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)\n$$\n\nIf we factor the given expression and use the quotient rule for logarithms, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\log_{5}(88)-5\\log_{5}(8)&=5\\bigl(\\log_{5}(88)-\\log_{5}(8)\\bigr) \\\\ &=5\\log_{5}\\left(\\frac{88}{8}\\right) \\\\ &=5\\log_{5}(11)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n5\\log_{5}(88)-5\\log_{5}(8)=5\\log_{5}(11)\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186607561 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1f2bb2c-df9c-5ae9-b367-cd44d12e64af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1f2bb2c-df9c-5ae9-b367-cd44d12e64af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607309} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9989fca-8bb3-5ac1-a1c8-80adaf3abb1a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607506}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "281450c9-dfc1-53b6-acba-a86432822f8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-318918" :question/problem "![](images/b9/b9439eb0a4a315d3603fd2ad264619081afdc1bbab5e42092fbff132a416329e.png)\n\nGiven the diagram above, where $|a|=6$ and $|b|=4\\sqrt{3},$ find the **exact** value of $|a+b|.$\n\n$|a+b|=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the triangle law for adding the vectors $a$ and $b.$\n\n![](images/20/20927a417004ed047a40f399566d5552f2b0fb9e243b483040a7d7d3af0cc73a.png)\n\nUsing the law of cosines, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a+b{|}^{2} & =|a{|}^{2}+|b{|}^{2}-2|a||b|\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘} \\\\  & ={6}^{2}+(4\\sqrt{3})^{2}-2\\cdot 6\\cdot (4\\sqrt{3})\\cdot \\,\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =36+48-72 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get\n\n$$\n|a+b|=2\\sqrt{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186607574 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdb85661-7ed9-53de-924e-872bc7641fe0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{91}{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdb85661-7ed9-53de-924e-872bc7641fe0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{91}{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607327} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7d14872-3340-5457-847f-ce7667c3d4c6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4a7183c5-b41e-5351-94e5-9c989303ac39" :question/math-academy-id "q-354033" :question/problem "Use the first two non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $\\sqrt{2x+1}$ about $x=4$ to approximate the value of $\\sqrt{9.2}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\sqrt{9.2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\sqrt{2x+1}$ and $a=4.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt{2x+1}=(2x+1)^{1/2} \\\\ f(4) & =\\sqrt{2(4)+1}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(2x+1)^{-1/2}\\cdot 2=(2x+1)^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & ={9}^{-1/2}=\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2x+1} & =3+\\frac{1}{3}(x-4)+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=4.1$ into the above to approximate $\\sqrt{9.2}.$ Since\n\n$$\n4.1-4=\\frac{1}{10},\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{9.2} & ≈3+\\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right) \\\\  & =3+\\frac{1}{30} \\\\  & =\\frac{91}{30}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186607576 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07c038a2-f3c7-5b2e-9bff-a211ec4c12ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607058} {:answer/id #uuid "4e1f7fb3-95d7-5a4b-b552-b22a41186fbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607151} {:answer/id #uuid "58f9a066-04f8-5e2f-92ae-80ad59ac13a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607166} {:answer/id #uuid "9b3a121d-8807-5294-afe8-68d0b3578b21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607283} {:answer/id #uuid "cdbb5a73-306d-5be8-a8d9-85363e988af5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607357}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdbb5a73-306d-5be8-a8d9-85363e988af5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607357} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a666bc1-5d84-5423-98fa-a134c0af2a70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "58b74103-40ca-5276-9059-d8731fdbe9a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-101556" :question/problem "$\\frac{1}{4}-\\left(-\\frac{3}{4}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "To subtract $-\\frac{3}{4}$ from $\\frac{1}{4}$, we first  combine the two negative signs to make a single plus sign. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}-\\left(-\\frac{3}{4}\\right) &=\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have a common denominator of $4$, so we combine the numerators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{1+3}{4} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we work out $1+3$ using our number line.\n\n![](images/b3/b33ec6a97d0dccd4e3532a4628e0d1e0672f582fd3519a6f82bce4496955f2c4.png)\n\nHence, \n$\\frac{1+3}{4}=\\frac{4}{4}=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186607577 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69b9e209-5cc9-557c-9dd7-6a88350a65ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69b9e209-5cc9-557c-9dd7-6a88350a65ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607192} :answer-field/id #uuid "aebeb642-e339-5c32-856f-49090fc0cf1e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607512}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5a564187-adae-534c-bb90-6e3dd9fd6d4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-186223" :question/problem "Expressed as a fraction in its simplest form, $\\frac{3}{4}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{8}\\right)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To subtract $-\\frac{1}{8}$ from $\\frac{3}{4}$, we drop the parentheses and combine the two negative signs to make a single plus sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{8}\\right)=\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{8} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the two numbers as fractions over a common denominator of $8$\n and combine the numerators. This gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{8}=\\frac{6}{8}+\\frac{1}{8}=\\frac{6+1}{8} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we work out $6+1$ using our number line.\n\n![](images/f0/f04b25d7f5164d0c5a456598ba8167b2476b0176a384af0e002c0dbc842634c5.png)\n\nSo we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{6+1}{8}=\\frac{7}{8}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186607587 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f98734f2-ead6-506f-9592-aab1a73b8bc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f98734f2-ead6-506f-9592-aab1a73b8bc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607426} :answer-field/id #uuid "09cb8856-371b-5dfb-b464-08311c3dd795" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607435} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "296f3ee1-93dd-5668-8f75-477f31cce0ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607104}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "296f3ee1-93dd-5668-8f75-477f31cce0ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607104} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c82430d-0d9e-5868-bdb0-1c00c4118b3e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607477} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7996a30e-6892-5819-830b-138bd7c82e6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7996a30e-6892-5819-830b-138bd7c82e6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607226} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3cfc86b-a00d-5ec2-a595-505c97f02e5f" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607535}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6d812880-789c-52fc-8b28-ef8d5436b5bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-329167" :question/problem "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\frac{4x-1}{1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\ge 0,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{4}<x<\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$x<$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}<x<\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\pi }{4} & <x<\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & <\\operatorname{tan}⁡x<\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\ -1 & <\\operatorname{tan}⁡x<1 \\\\ 1-1 & <1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x<1+1 \\\\ 0 & <1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x<2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is positive for all the values of $x$ that we are considering $(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}<x<\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ So, we can multiply both sides by $1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4x-1}{1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x} & \\ge 0 \\\\ (1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\cdot \\frac{4x-1}{1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x} & \\ge (1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 4x-1 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 4x & \\ge 1 \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality will be the numbers that satisfy both $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}<x<\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $x\\ge \\frac{1}{4}.$ So, the solution to our inequality is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{4}\\le x<\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186607599 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c19af68-d667-55d6-a6a0-ebb007fc0847" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}=0.585" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607171} {:answer/id #uuid "6328f666-7c70-580e-b80e-7e149e948f17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}=0.583" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607181} {:answer/id #uuid "6a4a2cf1-010f-5991-ab6e-fa0c77f33910" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}=0.574" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607193} {:answer/id #uuid "947f8650-d689-5843-afd9-3ca672d3b39a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}=0.578" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607269} {:answer/id #uuid "faa90d4a-e1fa-5881-aa51-7becbc4d1751" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}=0.568" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607427}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a4a2cf1-010f-5991-ab6e-fa0c77f33910" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}=0.574" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607193} :answer-field/id #uuid "85fdb8db-30cc-5e1a-85b2-c13d0810a491" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607494}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "85eb110b-2eb7-59d6-bcd2-e6e544398be7" :question/math-academy-id "q-126873" :question/problem "Use the first three terms of the Taylor expansion for $f(x)={x}^{-1/2}$ about $x=4$ to approximate the value of $1/\\sqrt{3}.$ Give your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=4.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{-1/2} \\\\ f(4) & =(4)^{-1/2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-3/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =-\\frac{1}{2}(4)^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{-5/2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(4) & =\\frac{3}{4}(4)^{-5/2}=\\frac{3}{128}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{-1/2} & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{16}(x-4)+\\frac{1}{2!}\\left(\\frac{3}{128}\\right)(x-4)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{16}(x-4)+\\frac{3}{256}(x-4)^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=3$ into the above to approximate $1/\\sqrt{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & ≈\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{16}(3-4)+\\frac{3}{256}(3-4)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{16}+\\frac{3}{256} \\\\  & =0.574\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186607601 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a8b81b1-3def-5703-a5e4-c2f4549bf4db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607256} {:answer/id #uuid "9a2283ce-c902-5f02-87fe-8b4476f33e28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{10}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607278} {:answer/id #uuid "9b241cc5-d761-558e-ba06-031580819423" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607282} {:answer/id #uuid "abda3278-6270-53ab-bd74-ba4b23108c60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607300} {:answer/id #uuid "da1b9d71-a51d-52cc-8030-beb31f1377d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da1b9d71-a51d-52cc-8030-beb31f1377d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607376} :answer-field/id #uuid "33b31dcb-148f-5c64-9798-464e3813562f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607451}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8a8300e6-c548-5c44-9e9f-8dddf24e8b0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-101558" :question/problem "$\\frac{5}{9}-\\left(-\\frac{2}{9}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "To subtract $-\\frac{2}{9}$ from $\\frac{5}{9}$, we first  combine the two negative signs to make a single plus sign. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{9}-\\left(-\\frac{2}{9}\\right) &=\\frac{5}{9}+\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have a common denominator of $9$, so we combine the numerators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{9}+\\frac{2}{9} &=\\frac{5+2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we work out $2+3$ using our number line.\n\n![](images/3a/3a55ed5ad957f34c73d067622479aeebad8c82d9c253fd51e9e70d6299c34bda.png)\n\nHence, \n$\\frac{5+2}{9}=\\frac{7}{9}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186607605 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5457eb34-6518-5417-adad-1b0b4dd6cd73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607160} {:answer/id #uuid "6b8bb4e0-e60a-5439-9257-556d45f1170c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607196} {:answer/id #uuid "6be617da-4fa3-58bd-b67f-ac46cabebbe1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607198} {:answer/id #uuid "7a2e58ad-28a9-5988-8f4d-fbe1fb5b18c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607228} {:answer/id #uuid "be5a7c3a-cad4-5d81-a2a7-a131d86e4588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a2e58ad-28a9-5988-8f4d-fbe1fb5b18c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607228} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e1645e0-faae-5c67-b4d9-d5c7b75d22d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607449}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "98b0ba23-fec6-5c8f-b838-63c988917078" :question/math-academy-id "q-123637" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)}=\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}=-1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186607608 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28a750d6-1086-5989-ae9f-4e92e529624f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28a750d6-1086-5989-ae9f-4e92e529624f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607103} :answer-field/id #uuid "62067e67-a1f7-575c-9e18-0dc893ff4875" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607479}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a419ddd9-5937-5da9-93f5-e3e5fc28b2af" :question/math-academy-id "q-186225" :question/problem "Expressed as a fraction in its simplest form, $-\\frac{5}{6}-\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To subtract $-\\frac{2}{3}$ from $-\\frac{5}{6}$, we drop the parentheses and combine the two negative signs to make a single plus sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{6}-\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right)=-\\frac{5}{6}+\\frac{2}{3} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the two numbers as fractions over a common denominator of $6$\n and combine the numerators. This gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{6}+\\frac{2}{3}=-\\frac{5}{6}+\\frac{4}{6}=\\frac{-5+4}{6} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we work out $-5+4$ using our number line.\n\n![](images/1b/1bce2773de6db6bfb754fb04afbe157700618372ac99a2f048854bf38784bc2e.png)\n\nSo we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{-5+4}{6}=-\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186607622 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03ad6588-ad87-5ca6-b185-3da1197b2cda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607052} {:answer/id #uuid "0d6212be-e8ce-5947-8304-18b7a0729b8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607063} {:answer/id #uuid "9e63b683-9d4a-58c5-b996-1cbafb7c7f87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607286} {:answer/id #uuid "b0b9fbb9-72fe-59a9-9e48-42c9dd0226a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607308} {:answer/id #uuid "ed2a1838-a5ad-5d0c-9570-375eed3b5c34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607407}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed2a1838-a5ad-5d0c-9570-375eed3b5c34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607407} :answer-field/id #uuid "589b17d4-9157-5858-aec7-af7638bd6435" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607472}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bc3d7b04-1d0c-5ed8-b773-eb655aa42e54" :question/math-academy-id "q-75400" :question/problem "$\\frac{3}{2}-\\left(-\\frac{5}{4}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "To subtract $-\\frac{5}{4}$ from $\\frac{3}{2}$, we drop  the parentheses and combine the two negative signs to make a single plus sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}-\\left(-\\frac{5}{4}\\right)=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{5}{4} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the two numbers as fractions over a common denominator of $4$\n and combine the numerators. This gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{5}{4}=\\frac{6}{4}+\\frac{5}{6}=\\frac{6+5}{4} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we work out $6+5$ using our number line.\n\n![](images/8c/8c3dee6cf43c19e390225c52dfa7ce7d5d420b9666d6559f3bfe6cbfc3c13750.png)\n\nSo we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{6+5}{4}=\\frac{11}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186607640 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e74f605-370c-5c20-bd1b-34d0007344ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607153} {:answer/id #uuid "c5e4a1ca-5d8a-5574-b6d3-810ed3adcfaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607346} {:answer/id #uuid "d340a15e-66fe-5784-80a4-443107258c64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607365} {:answer/id #uuid "ed5583a0-c0dd-5880-84af-819bdb431cb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607408} {:answer/id #uuid "eeb23aa7-441a-54b9-be44-c6d625e8d0a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5e4a1ca-5d8a-5574-b6d3-810ed3adcfaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607346} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3e524c8-2898-5c1d-8dc0-d61ee9a8e699" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607522}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f6746ea3-d6c1-5e0b-abfc-e06173c8ac14" :question/math-academy-id "q-68685" :question/problem "What number can be added to $-\\frac{3}{4}$ to get a sum of $0?$" :question/worked-solution "The number that can be added to $-\\frac{3}{4}$ to give $0$ is the additive inverse of $-\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nTo find the additive inverse of the number $-\\frac{3}{4}$, we change its sign by placing a minus symbol $(-)$ in front of it. So, we obtain \n\n$$\n-\\,\\left(-\\frac{3}{4}\\right)=\\frac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nTo check our work, we simply add the inverse to the original number. If we're correct, we should get zero.\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\frac{3}{4}\\right)+\\frac{3}{4}=-\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{3}{4}=0\\,✓\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186607642 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3400c772-cc12-5c6c-9f29-a713bcf321e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,\\text{mm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607116} {:answer/id #uuid "5534a4b4-f5c9-53f8-aebd-b6bd24a7282e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{mm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607162} {:answer/id #uuid "65844e6a-84c0-5652-9a2b-ea655a7e3bbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{mm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607185} {:answer/id #uuid "871b491c-e5e1-5d43-89e9-a1825bd670c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}\\,\\text{mm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607251} {:answer/id #uuid "b4403f9f-b97e-5876-85b1-c4c68b072da9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{mm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607314}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3400c772-cc12-5c6c-9f29-a713bcf321e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,\\text{mm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607116} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c5831f7-bf6c-51c6-8a8a-68d352946d5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fa4f8320-6b7a-589e-9bc6-89656a2ac0ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-118423" :question/problem "Calculate the diameter of a sphere given that its volume is $\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\,{\\text{mm}}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "We can find the volume of a sphere using the formula\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the sphere.\n\nSubstituting $V=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\,{\\text{mm}}^{3}$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{6} & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\pi \\cdot 3}{6\\cdot 4\\pi } & ={r}^{3} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{2}^{3}} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nr=\\sqrt[3]{{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{mm}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the diameter of the sphere is\n\n$$\nd=2r=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=1\\,\\text{mm}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608164 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1eb2f314-6218-5d20-846d-b1f62c244921" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607839} {:answer/id #uuid "1f4d7f16-9e28-5cde-9ed7-301d3003cbf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607841} {:answer/id #uuid "4b379efb-0a72-500a-8ebf-e235cb2af673" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607901} {:answer/id #uuid "bafabf47-e243-501e-acbd-67f3d795684d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608015} {:answer/id #uuid "fdc1e9c9-25e8-5c9a-a2fb-c81db3158f1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608088}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bafabf47-e243-501e-acbd-67f3d795684d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608015} :answer-field/id #uuid "f92ab846-6ecb-5402-adcb-73ad9f5b7d61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "08c4a96f-48cd-5998-9cd6-61ef07570afd" :question/math-academy-id "q-49536" :question/problem "Find the value of ${\\int }_{0}^{1/10}\\frac{15}{\\sqrt{1-25{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1/10}\\frac{15}{\\sqrt{1-25{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=15{\\int }_{0}^{1/10}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(5x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $(5x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=5x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{10}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/10}\\frac{15}{\\sqrt{1-25{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =15{\\int }_{0}^{1/10}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(5x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =15{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-3\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608168 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f888669-2477-534b-93c1-14dd41a1467c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{{e}^{z}+1}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607822} {:answer/id #uuid "8a28e55e-aa6e-55ec-9c6f-983a66c3f811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{e}^{z}+1}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607968} {:answer/id #uuid "a422661a-62b6-548f-99fb-b5e248bf15ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{e}^{z}+1}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607999} {:answer/id #uuid "dd7625c3-f004-5df2-addc-77e2835786c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{e}^{z}+2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608048} {:answer/id #uuid "ef774d49-b81e-572f-9499-ca77b619a297" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{e}^{z}+1}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f888669-2477-534b-93c1-14dd41a1467c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{{e}^{z}+1}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607822} :answer-field/id #uuid "060253c5-cf85-5b59-9850-bd847d9b4cdb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "14a57d7f-2f51-563e-9400-c8274f6aaa38" :question/math-academy-id "q-49574" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{{e}^{z}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{z}+1}}\\,\\text{d}z.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's introduce the new variable $u={e}^{z}+1.$ Differentiating gives \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}={e}^{z}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u={e}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{e}^{z}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{z}+1}}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{z}+1}}\\cdot {e}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{1/2}}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{e}^{z}+1}+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608178 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34c67ac0-41e8-5678-8336-d0107e58afba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-{6}^{x}|}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607865} {:answer/id #uuid "4d4864b2-2c2f-57f4-956b-9433730346bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-{6}^{x}|}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607904} {:answer/id #uuid "70f388a5-6722-5425-b634-fd9fcace933e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-{6}^{x}|}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607939} {:answer/id #uuid "7e2d864c-a850-55c1-893b-e69551329f80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-{6}^{x}|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607958} {:answer/id #uuid "8f824d94-ffac-51cd-ade0-eac66ce2397f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-{6}^{x}|}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34c67ac0-41e8-5678-8336-d0107e58afba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-{6}^{x}|}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607865} :answer-field/id #uuid "be0455fc-1523-58f5-9e10-d164fbc5cf2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608146}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "35f0c1cb-49ca-5544-8236-0e4cefc5c8cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-113875" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{{6}^{x}}{2-{6}^{x}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the numerator is proportional to the derivative of the denominator. So, we introduce the new variable $u=2-{6}^{x}.$ Differentiating gives \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{6}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡6\\,⟹\\,-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}\\,\\text{d}u={6}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{6}^{x}}{2-{6}^{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2-{6}^{x}}\\cdot {6}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}\\right)\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-{6}^{x}|}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608184 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4509901e-f9a9-51bd-9843-b72e2a5ea1a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607893} {:answer/id #uuid "5718c096-0547-5c20-b5b5-e3ffff431e40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "160" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607916} {:answer/id #uuid "7dbadb83-9110-548f-a0c8-d349a3b35f24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607956} {:answer/id #uuid "995fe3be-5ba6-5c96-b9e5-2b6ee89bced3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607987} {:answer/id #uuid "f6657d71-5e2e-5c0f-96e0-670bfc63ca39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "140" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7dbadb83-9110-548f-a0c8-d349a3b35f24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607956} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0b50335-1f15-5732-ae6d-39d62e0ce446" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608139}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "46fc820e-4197-5e9b-9901-eb94f384763a" :question/math-academy-id "q-124589" :question/problem "Evaluate the series ${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }25{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $2.$ However, it is still a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{2},$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nFor the first few terms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & =25{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{2}=16 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =25{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{3}=\\frac{64}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{64}{5}\\right)}{16}=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }25{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{(1-\\frac{4}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)} \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608189 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ade5a23-8cac-5f6c-9e1e-d06a283f53a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.57" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607900} {:answer/id #uuid "7a0637a1-7bf3-500d-add8-4dd7574465f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607953} {:answer/id #uuid "a0baf4f6-8b9b-52dd-a085-9abbc2c09e6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607995} {:answer/id #uuid "b0c4cb53-e0d1-53e9-aca5-e9a8a57be477" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608011} {:answer/id #uuid "e342688f-bbba-56ff-8771-4fccd900294a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.68" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608053}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0c4cb53-e0d1-53e9-aca5-e9a8a57be477" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608011} :answer-field/id #uuid "28e23342-5157-52c1-a103-d2f8cade008c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608095}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5842a4d5-df96-50bb-ae16-8e81cc3dcbfe" :question/math-academy-id "q-345150" :question/problem "![](images/b5/b5603a9a67072aad7aa18715715a0899bf47add1f165a02831db9f2282e5021a.png)\n\nFind, in amperes, the amplitude of the steady state current for the RCL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $20\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $\\frac{1}{24}\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $4\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{(1/24)}=24q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=4\\,\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dq}{dt}\\right)=4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+24q+4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+6q=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+6{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-3t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))+5(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\,\\, &  \\\\ +\\,6(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ (2\\alpha +10\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+(-10\\alpha +2\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha +10\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ -10\\alpha +2\\beta &=\\frac{5}{2}\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{25}{104},$ $\\beta =\\frac{5}{104}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{25}{104}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{5}{104}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-3t}-\\frac{25}{104}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{5}{104}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-3t}-\\frac{25}{104}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{5}{104}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =-2A{e}^{-2t}-3B{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{25}{52}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{5}{52}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=-2A{e}^{-2t}-3B{e}^{-3t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{25}{52}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{5}{52}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{25}{52}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{5}{52}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{25}{52}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{5}{52}\\right)}^{2}}≈0.49,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608197 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39d5ef65-180d-54bb-b652-f04fd78978d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{125(x+1)^3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39d5ef65-180d-54bb-b652-f04fd78978d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{125(x+1)^3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607871} :answer-field/id #uuid "c24428f5-e3c0-55c7-8078-749b7acf7485" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608150}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "791efa13-58a3-5d22-b346-34fe6122e592" :question/math-academy-id "q-277070" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{4}{5\\sqrt[3]{x}}-1.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{4}{5\\sqrt[3]{x}}-1\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{4}{5\\sqrt[3]{y}}-1.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{4}{5\\sqrt[3]{y}}-1 \\\\ \\frac{4}{5\\sqrt[3]{y}} & =x+1 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{y} & =\\frac{4}{5(x+1)} \\\\ {(\\sqrt[3]{y})}^{3} & ={\\left(\\frac{4}{5(x+1)}\\right)}^{3} \\\\ y & =\\frac{64}{125(x+1)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{64}{125(x+1)^{3}}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne 0,\\,f(x)\\ne -1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne -1,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)\\ne 0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608200 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72b294fd-feeb-5d91-a485-bd4ca6e7ff5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72b294fd-feeb-5d91-a485-bd4ca6e7ff5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607943} :answer-field/id #uuid "de2a2410-e3f8-55ae-881a-4ed3bf6cefc5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608160}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "803e7fdf-89be-5ced-89f5-50ee78e6a31a" :question/math-academy-id "q-352597" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{n}.\n$$\n\nThe series converges to $f(x)$ for $|x|<R,$ where $R$ is the radius of convergence of the Taylor series. Calculate the radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ about $x=0.$\n\nThe radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of ${f}^{′}(x),$ we need to compute $R.$\n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{n+1}}{{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{x}{2}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$ So, it converges when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{|x|}{2} & <1 \\\\ |x| & <2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is $R=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608213 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4905c93-59e4-5489-bd39-01a851d21814" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608000}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4905c93-59e4-5489-bd39-01a851d21814" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608000} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0dee006-775c-5be5-9366-3859c81c1fc2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a9da4d62-79c8-546a-8cfa-f2f42789b365" :question/math-academy-id "q-351854" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}\\frac{4}{5+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}\\frac{4}{5+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}\\frac{4}{5\\cdot (1+{x}^{2}/5)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}\\frac{1}{1+(x/\\sqrt{5})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{5}}\\right)}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{5}}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt{5}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}\\frac{4}{5+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{4}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}\\frac{1}{1+(x/\\sqrt{5})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{5}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}\\,\\pi }{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608217 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10252354-59e0-52a9-9b48-74493655f2f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607823} {:answer/id #uuid "14ee31c6-4603-5df0-8b74-6be6e55eac1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607828} {:answer/id #uuid "4f98b232-1df5-5b21-af96-8e7b1948133d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607909} {:answer/id #uuid "71d9a123-911c-547c-8814-a2abefd2a870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{49\\pi }{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607941} {:answer/id #uuid "eb6848f6-e949-5df8-aef3-41b88ea3c1cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{49\\pi }{80}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608062}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10252354-59e0-52a9-9b48-74493655f2f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607823} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ba83c09-1b1f-54ba-93e5-b8d879de48d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b356033c-192a-55fa-8af3-a9a824c7a21b" :question/math-academy-id "q-19398" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{7}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{7}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{7}{4\\cdot (1+{x}^{2}/4)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}/4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{1+(x/2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=2\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{7}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{7}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{1+(x/2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (2\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)-\\frac{7}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608225 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "320b020b-77f2-5ea7-bf62-812fa51af47b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607859} {:answer/id #uuid "4707afc2-b0a1-5103-8d80-c0d02ffe74c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607894} {:answer/id #uuid "a8311efe-6cb0-5710-92a0-dabc4de43258" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608004} {:answer/id #uuid "ef2e530c-c8ac-5ab5-b188-0b2e2231eadf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608068} {:answer/id #uuid "fbc4518d-2c8b-5f8f-807a-719ac2f9936b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef2e530c-c8ac-5ab5-b188-0b2e2231eadf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608068} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f945d0e-7d66-5bae-a734-9a463648e5b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d26ac45d-58ea-5f3f-9bee-0a0453127a2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-5361" :question/problem "Evaluate the geometric series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-4){\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{n+2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nThe first term is \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=(-4){\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{1+2}=(-4){\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{3}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the second term is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=(-4){\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{2+2}=(-4){\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common ratio is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{(-4){\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{4}}{(-4){\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-4){\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{n+2} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-4){\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{3}}{1-\\frac{1}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-4){\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{3}}{\\frac{4}{5}} \\\\  & =(-4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{{5}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{5}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608226 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e6bd806-89c4-5060-8d23-9bed6fb26dba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=\\frac{6}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607877} {:answer/id #uuid "87991296-ccc9-53b7-b7d8-1bb5c457adb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=\\frac{12}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607964} {:answer/id #uuid "8fbcf31a-e7c9-586b-b944-bf042694b0c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607972} {:answer/id #uuid "bac828bc-36f7-5e79-a09d-7467c3b4d8c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=\\frac{6}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608014} {:answer/id #uuid "d654cc50-c653-543e-8fda-5878edc1927a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{1}{2}q=\\frac{6}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608042}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bac828bc-36f7-5e79-a09d-7467c3b4d8c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=\\frac{6}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608014} :answer-field/id #uuid "76f07b5d-aa82-54fe-a7c6-78be352ffdeb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608118}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d3a73495-4242-5bc3-8f84-a0e85455e4e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-345101" :question/problem "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $50\\,Ω,$ a capacitance of $0.02\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductance of $L=25\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "We have the following circuit diagram.\n\n![](images/47/4748031fddc6d6c6489ed6575178c9105130756a72060db16ea494d8dd0f7c59.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=50\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=50I=50\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.02}=50q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=25\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=25\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=25\\,\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dq}{dt}\\right)=25\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n6=50\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+50q+25\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=\\frac{6}{25}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608231 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ee0e34e-f6d5-5af6-b368-785e125dd5ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=14u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607820} {:answer/id #uuid "300779fb-3ca8-50ec-8881-e6eda6045d93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=7u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607858} {:answer/id #uuid "33ddce50-bc0f-5411-b865-63ea9d758b9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{2}{7}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607864} {:answer/id #uuid "4e6601fc-1228-5c88-913c-95151af46bed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{7}{2}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607907} {:answer/id #uuid "8460eb55-8574-5953-8d6c-455b0922f8de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{14}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8460eb55-8574-5953-8d6c-455b0922f8de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{14}u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607960} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2c66f2e-6954-59df-885e-9bcb0a5d8c53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eaeb8059-b927-502e-8a95-3fc7550509e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-115571" :question/problem "If $t$ varies directly with $u$ and $t=\\frac{1}{2}$ when $u=7$, find the relationship between $u$ and $t.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $t$ varies directly with $u,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\nt=ku.\n$$\n\nNext, we use the fact that $t=\\frac{1}{2}$ when $u=7$ to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & =k\\cdot 7 \\\\ \\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{7} & =k \\\\ \\frac{1}{2\\cdot 7} & =k \\\\ \\frac{1}{14} & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our direct variation equation is\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{1}{14}u.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608944 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "183a6b38-2b46-52ea-9809-16ab745ca791" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{3}^{8}\\times {\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608458} {:answer/id #uuid "642ed4e7-c647-53a7-b68e-1d21f8cdd396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{3}^{8}\\times 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608583} {:answer/id #uuid "8db90444-ce84-5767-b308-a4836aea30f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\times {\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608637} {:answer/id #uuid "bebc9e30-b803-5154-84fe-92f90f0fb4f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{3}^{8}\\times \\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608733} {:answer/id #uuid "f109a3b0-ed8d-53a2-ae98-a462b4475e25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{3}^{8}\\times 8}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608813}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "183a6b38-2b46-52ea-9809-16ab745ca791" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{3}^{8}\\times {\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608458} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9421c28-a448-5c50-9112-ff64a46510d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608904}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "02212d6f-6c9b-5a4d-aa9d-7acdf8e69df5" :question/math-academy-id "q-100655" :question/problem "${(3\\times \\frac{1}{4})}^{8}=$" :question/worked-solution "To raise the product of two numbers to the power of $8$, we can raise each number to the power of $8,$ and then multiply:\n\n$$\n{(3\\times \\frac{1}{4})}^{8}={3}^{8}\\times {\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{8}\n$$"}]
